WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.643 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Put a number on each end of this line.

2
00:00:02.643 --> 00:00:06.191 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Replace each number by the number at its neighbour.

3
00:00:06.191 --> 00:00:12.104 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Two numbers simply swap places. This tiny operation will
lead to a question about how

4
00:00:12.104 --> 00:00:13.704 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
large a network can be.

5
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.343 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A graph is a collection of dots joined by lines.

6
00:00:17.343 --> 00:00:22.477 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Its adjacency table has a one when two dots are joined and a
zero otherwise.

7
00:00:22.477 --> 00:00:27.612 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Multiplying by this table means adding the numbers at each
dot's neighbours.

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.400 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Start with one at each end. After the swap, nothing changes.

9
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.920 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The pattern has been multiplied by positive one.

10
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:42.520 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A pattern that only gets scaled is an eigenvector, and the
scale factor is its eigenvalue.

11
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.151 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Now start with one and minus one. The swap reverses the
signs.

12
00:00:47.151 --> 00:00:50.649 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The new pattern is minus one times the old pattern.

13
00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:54.900 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
This gives an eigenvalue of negative one: the direction
flips.

14
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.135 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A graph with n dots has n eigenvalues, counting repeats.

15
00:00:59.135 --> 00:01:03.519 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Because its adjacency table is symmetric, they are real
numbers.

16
00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:06.943 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
We count how many are positive, zero and negative.

17
00:01:06.943 --> 00:01:09.820 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
These three counts are called the inertia.

18
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.092 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A triangle has eigenvalues two, minus one, minus one.

19
00:01:14.092 --> 00:01:19.820 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
One positive and two negative. A pentagon has three positive
and two negative.

20
00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:24.960 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
So the same negative count can coexist with different
positive counts.

21
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:29.748 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
We fix the negative count at four. How many positive values
are possible?

22
00:01:29.748 --> 00:01:34.950 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
And how many dots can a network have? Those are different
questions, because some dots

23
00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.941 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
can add zero eigenvalues without adding positive or negative
ones.

24
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:44.759 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Copy one endpoint of our original edge, giving the copy
exactly the same neighbours.

25
00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:49.345 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Put one on the original, minus one on the copy, and zero in
the middle.

26
00:01:49.345 --> 00:01:53.349 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Every neighbour sum is zero. The copied difference
disappears.

27
00:01:53.733 --> 00:02:00.142 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Copies leave the positive and negative counts unchanged,
though the individual eigenvalues

28
00:02:00.142 --> 00:02:03.276 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
may change. Lonely dots also add only zeros.

29
00:02:03.276 --> 00:02:08.901 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
To ask a finite size question, remove these easy ways of
making a graph bigger.

30
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.667 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A reduced graph has no lonely dots and no two dots with
identical sets of neighbours.

31
00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.319 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Reduced does not mean no zero eigenvalues.

32
00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:22.812 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Other linear dependencies can still create zeros, and they
matter for our size problem.

33
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.574 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The answer for four negatives is thirty dots in a reduced
graph.

34
00:02:27.574 --> 00:02:30.375 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Exactly four reduced graphs reach thirty.

35
00:02:30.375 --> 00:02:35.227 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Each has four positive, twenty-two zero, and four negative
eigenvalues.

36
00:02:35.227 --> 00:02:40.216 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The zeros explain why thirty dots need not mean thirty
active directions.

37
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.471 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The largest possible positive count is eleven, achieved by
two reduced graphs with fifteen

38
00:02:44.471 --> 00:02:48.342 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
dots and no zeros. The numerical bound and these examples
were already supplied by earlier

39
00:02:48.342 --> 00:02:51.568 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
work. The classification proves that no other reduced graph
attains eleven.

40
00:02:51.967 --> 00:02:55.049 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
How can a finite computation settle all graphs?

41
00:02:55.049 --> 00:03:01.213 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
First keep an induced core with the same positive and
negative counts but no zero eigenvalues.

42
00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:06.919 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Induced means that, among the dots you keep, you retain
every original connecting line.

43
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.265 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The number of dots in a core is its rank.

44
00:03:10.265 --> 00:03:17.063 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
With no zero eigenvalues, its table is nonsingular: an
inverse table can undo the calculation.

45
00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:23.500 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Reduced graphs can be larger than their cores, so we must
add zero directions back later.

46
00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:29.978 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The second ingredient says every nonsingular graph can be
built from a smaller one by

47
00:03:29.978 --> 00:03:36.088 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
adding one dot, or sometimes two. Two are essential: our
first edge has a nonsingular

48
00:03:36.088 --> 00:03:39.971 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
two-by-two table, while a lone dot has the zero table.

49
00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:45.813 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Why does the rule cover every case? Jacobi's determinant
identity examines the inverse

50
00:03:45.813 --> 00:03:51.260 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
table. Either a nonzero diagonal entry permits one deletion,
or a nonzero off-diagonal

51
00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:56.327 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
entry permits two. The paper proves these alternatives leave
a nonsingular core.

52
00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.819 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For each core, try every possible zero-or-one list of
connections to a new dot.

53
00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:05.578 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A calculation using the inverse tells which sign is added.

54
00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:10.956 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Written with the determinant and adjugate, its sign is
decided using whole numbers.

55
00:04:11.333 --> 00:04:16.637 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Watch a concrete case. Join the new dot to both ends of the
edge, making a triangle.

56
00:04:16.637 --> 00:04:22.509 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The connection list is one, one. The inverse table is the
same swap table, so the calculation

57
00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:26.549 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
gives two. Its negative contributes one new negative
eigenvalue.

58
00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:32.989 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The program grows every allowed graph, merges copies made by
renaming, and discards anything

59
00:04:32.989 --> 00:04:37.729 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
exceeding four negatives. It also tries the required two-dot
extensions.

60
00:04:37.729 --> 00:04:42.797 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
This is exhaustive generation guided by a lemma, rather than
a random search.

61
00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:47.944 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For exactly four negatives, the number of cores rises and
then falls.

62
00:04:47.944 --> 00:04:53.759 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
At fifteen dots, just two remain. At sixteen and seventeen,
none remain even when we

63
00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:59.367 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
allow fewer than four negatives. These empty levels are the
stopping certificate.

64
00:04:59.767 --> 00:05:04.308 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Why are two empty levels enough? Suppose there were a first
larger core.

65
00:05:04.308 --> 00:05:06.767 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Remove one or two dots using the lemma.

66
00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:09.857 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Its parent would have to sit on an earlier level.

67
00:05:09.857 --> 00:05:13.831 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Starting from two empty levels, neither route can ever
restart.

68
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:19.704 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The complete census contains forty-five thousand two hundred
and forty-two cores with

69
00:05:19.704 --> 00:05:22.942 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
at most four negatives. Two implementations agree.

70
00:05:22.942 --> 00:05:28.899 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Exact replay checks every recorded graph and its sign
counts, and the mathematical extension

71
00:05:28.899 --> 00:05:31.360 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
rule is what establishes completeness.

72
00:05:31.733 --> 00:05:34.951 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Now reconstruct a larger reduced graph from a core.

73
00:05:34.951 --> 00:05:38.484 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Each extra dot gets a nonzero binary connection pattern.

74
00:05:38.484 --> 00:05:42.079 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Its self-calculation must be zero to avoid changing rank.

75
00:05:42.079 --> 00:05:47.189 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Between two added dots, the corresponding calculation must
be either zero or one.

76
00:05:47.567 --> 00:05:52.735 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Compatible patterns can coexist. An exact search finds the
largest mutually compatible

77
00:05:52.735 --> 00:05:57.664 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
collection for every core. This is the method of Akbari,
Cameron and Khosrovshahi.

78
00:05:57.664 --> 00:06:01.871 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Applying it across all relevant ranks supplies the missing
size bound.

79
00:06:02.267 --> 00:06:07.004 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Here are the maximum reduced sizes for ranks five through
fifteen.

80
00:06:07.004 --> 00:06:12.891 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The peak is thirty, at rank eight. Above rank eight, the
maxima fall below thirty.

81
00:06:12.891 --> 00:06:18.131 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Excluding those higher ranks is the central new step in the
size theorem.

82
00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:22.038 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Thirty is actually attainable. Start with one edge.

83
00:06:22.038 --> 00:06:24.952 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Copy every dot and its connection pattern.

84
00:06:24.952 --> 00:06:29.877 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Join a new dot to all copies, then attach one more dot to
that new dot.

85
00:06:29.877 --> 00:06:31.820 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The old two dots become six.

86
00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:36.477 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Repeat the same operation: six becomes fourteen, then
thirty.

87
00:06:36.477 --> 00:06:41.946 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A second version adds connections from the final new dot to
the original dots.

88
00:06:41.946 --> 00:06:48.256 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Using the variant first and the ordinary version afterwards
gives the four maximum graphs.

89
00:06:48.633 --> 00:06:53.528 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Those four rank-eight classes were already known to Akbari,
Cameron and Khosrovshahi

90
00:06:53.528 --> 00:06:58.598 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
in two thousand and four. This work proves that allowing
other ranks with four negative

91
00:06:58.598 --> 00:07:02.793 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
eigenvalues does not produce a bigger graph or another
thirty-dot class.

92
00:07:03.167 --> 00:07:07.384 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For the eleven-positive record, the first graph is called W
five.

93
00:07:07.384 --> 00:07:10.368 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Start with five mutually connected point dots.

94
00:07:10.368 --> 00:07:12.834 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Add a dot for each of their ten pairs.

95
00:07:12.834 --> 00:07:18.479 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Join each pair dot to its two points, and join pair dots
when their pairs are disjoint.

96
00:07:18.867 --> 00:07:22.315 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The other graph, X, begins with a three-by-three grid.

97
00:07:22.315 --> 00:07:27.807 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Join points sharing a row or column. Add six dots for the
wrap-around diagonals in the

98
00:07:27.807 --> 00:07:33.171 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
two directions. Join each to its three points, and join
parallel line dots together.

99
00:07:33.567 --> 00:07:37.654 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Each graph has fifteen eigenvalues: eleven positive and four
negative.

100
00:07:37.654 --> 00:07:42.383 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Their degree patterns differ, so they cannot be the same
graph with renamed dots.

101
00:07:42.383 --> 00:07:47.638 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The completed census and reconstruction checks show that
these are the only reduced graphs

102
00:07:47.638 --> 00:07:49.215 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
attaining eleven positives.

103
00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:54.821 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Charles, Farber, Johnson and Kennedy-Shaffer supplied both
examples and the numerical

104
00:07:54.821 --> 00:07:57.769 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
bound underlying this result in twenty thirteen.

105
00:07:57.769 --> 00:08:02.929 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The later inertia identity of Elphick and colleagues makes
the implication explicit.

106
00:08:02.929 --> 00:08:07.720 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The retained increment here is uniqueness, alongside
independent verification.

107
00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:13.377 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Keep the two records separate. Thirty is the maximum number
of dots after copies and

108
00:08:13.377 --> 00:08:18.717 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
isolated dots are removed. Eleven is the maximum positive
count, with four negatives.

109
00:08:18.717 --> 00:08:23.052 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The size champions and positive-count champions are
different graphs.

110
00:08:23.433 --> 00:08:28.548 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The proof combines written linear-algebra arguments with
complete exact computations.

111
00:08:28.548 --> 00:08:31.076 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
It is not a proof-assistant formalisation.

112
00:08:31.076 --> 00:08:36.973 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Integer and rational tests make the decisions; decimal
eigenvalue pictures are only illustrations.

113
00:08:36.973 --> 00:08:39.801 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Both the lemmas and the computation are needed.

114
00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:45.192 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
For five negative eigenvalues, the corresponding
maximum-size question remains open.

115
00:08:45.192 --> 00:08:48.282 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
A pattern in the small cases is a clue, not a proof.

116
00:08:48.282 --> 00:08:53.392 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
Failed searches through selected examples cannot replace the
exhaustive generation and

117
00:08:53.392 --> 00:08:55.056 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
stopping argument used here.

118
00:08:55.433 --> 00:09:01.174 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
You have seen the whole route: moving numbers gives
eigenvalues; removing zero directions

119
00:09:01.174 --> 00:09:06.206 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
gives cores; complete generation and compatible
reconstruction give the bound.

120
00:09:06.206 --> 00:09:11.753 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
The paper and verification files contain the full arguments,
exact census and explicit

121
00:09:11.753 --> 00:09:12.785 line:2% position:50% size:94% align:center
extremal graphs.
